    "body": "<div class=\"nigfacts\" style=\"--fb:#D0E8C0;--fbr:#8ECD64;--ftx:#375920\"><div class=\"nigfact\" style=\"--fc:#00446f\"><img class=\"fe fe-img\" alt=\"\" src=\"/resources/assets/img/a5b672aa1dfbe68b.png\"><span class=\"ft\">First sung at the Rebbe’s farbrengen on (,” 1965)</span></div><div class=\"nigfact\" style=\"--fc:#00446f\"><img class=\"fe fe-img\" alt=\"\" src=\"/resources/assets/img/a5b672aa1dfbe68b.png\"><span class=\"ft\">First sung by Reb Michoel Dvorkin in the</span></div><div class=\"nigfact\" style=\"--fc:#00446f\"><img class=\"fe fe-img\" alt=\"\" src=\"/resources/assets/img/09171c7832fadd54.png\"><span class=\"ft\">The Rebbe requested that this niggun be sung by all the chayolim at the Tzivos Hashem rally</span></div></div><h3>Nothing Besides Hashem</h3><p>\"We know Russian!\" Yochi giggles. \"Nyet nyet nikavo!\"</p><p>She and her twin brother Yossi sing at the top of their lungs, clickety-clacking their rhythm sticks as they march around the dining room table.</p><p>\"Yes, this is a Russian niggun,\" calls their older sister Sara, who is talking to Esther Miriam on the couch. \"Did you know that from the time of the Frierdiker Rebbe, until just forty years ago, the Russian government didn’t let people teach about Hashem?\"</p><p>Russia now has Chabad Houses, and summer camps, and shuls where everyone can learn how there really is nothing besides Hashem.\"</p><p>\"That didn't stop us Chassidim,\" declares Tzvi, putting down his piccolo as he marches behind the twins. \"We sang proudly—in Russian—that there is nothing besides Hashem.\"</p><p>\"That’s a sign that Moshiach’s mamesh almost here,\" says Tzvi, grinning back.</p><p>\"Yeah!\" Sara grins. “We used their own language to talk about Hashem—and that’s powerful!</p><p>\"Totally geuladik!” Esther Miriam calls out, grabbing her tambourine and joining the march.</p>",
    "source": "Source: Parshas Yisro · 21 Shvat 5787"
  },
